ormation in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ can be represented as a $3\\times 3$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $\\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j},$ and $\\mathbf k$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060116/assets/7a94c68b63e914bc3bbc64fb22bfe85ad60746bd2f35cd1dce877f979067c39a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060116/example-5438.json" "file_sha256" "e646b4bb6f465664f84838a5dce5c710f695f81eeb85207fa4d742bf0bdbfc2d"} {"question" "e-5437" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In the diagram below, $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf j$ are the vectors of the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$, while $\\mathbf{i'}$ and $\\mathbf{j'}$ are their images under the linear transformation $\\mathbf T.$\nWhat is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf T?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060116/assets/4d1b9e56c0c6214d949d12f45fe4aecea5516971203724ecf573a2b17c6e959a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060116/example-5437.json" "file_sha256" "6a9d33bad5a27facf0bbd05b86bef47fac1f1c4c0fc02e6d2db98f28e176a783"} {"question" "e-5437" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Every linear transformation in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nFrom the diagram we see that\n\n- the image of $\\mathbf{i}$ is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060116/assets/ccf1d5e4e5f040782f606fcccc9e183d92a640d4b5148602ce54b3e75ed116a5.png) and\n\n- the image of $\\mathbf{j}$ is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060116/assets/7746aad7a72ef32c38d2851200542f13c7ca8c2c805580b2cee4f78bbd1d0bed.png)\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060116/assets/0dfa1b41bac9f0d8250950b3fc538799b1f94271793aef7c13f3f7ce6aaf53ea.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060116/example-5437.json" "file_sha256" "6a9d33bad5a27facf0bbd05b86bef47fac1f1c4c0fc02e6d2db98f28e176a783"} {"question" "e-5436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ has the standard matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060116/assets/343c9e46cf59cf4372a563c80dd9010bcece8c04f8babdb95d57c04cdf4a2503.png) \nWhat are the images of the standard basis vectors $\\{ \\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j} \\}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ under the action of $\\mathbf T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060116/example-5436.json" "file_sha256" "9b50626db120a70fc1b2f793f85843ce287079c85e0b60102d9fe4fab60860c1"} {"question" "e-5436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Method 1**\n\nThe images of the vectors in the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j} \\}$ are the first and second columns of $T,$ respectively. \n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =T[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T(j) & =T[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the images of the vectors in the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j} \\}$ by computing the matrix products $T\\cdot\\mathbf{i}$ and $T\\cdot\\mathbf{j}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =T\\cdot i=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T(j) & =T\\cdot j=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060116/example-5436.json" "file_sha256" "9b50626db120a70fc1b2f793f85843ce287079c85e0b60102d9fe4fab60860c1"}] "usage" {"e-5437" [] "e-5438" [] "e-5436" [] "q-43894" []} "basis" 2038 "retractions" [[17592186134356 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2211/Source/Images/q-43894.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $i$ and $j$ are the vectors of the standard basis of $R^{2}$, while $i^{′}$ and $j^{′}$ are their images under the linear transformation $T$.\nWhat is the standard matrix of $T$?"] [17592186133486 :question/problem "Given that $\\{\\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j}, \\mathbf k\\}$ is the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the images of $\\mathbf{i}$, $\\mathbf{j}$, and $\\mathbf{k}$ under the action of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ are\n$\\langle 3, 5, -1 \\rangle$, $\\langle 0, 4,3 \\rangle$, and $\\langle -1, 5, -2\\rangle$, respectively, what is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$?"] [17592186133486 :question/worked-solution "Every linear transformation in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ can be represented as a $3\\times 3$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $\\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j}$, and $\\mathbf k$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186133485 :question/problem "In the diagram below, $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf j$ are the vectors of the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$, while $\\mathbf{i'}$ and $\\mathbf{j'}$ are their images under the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$.\nWhat is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf T$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2211/Source/Images/9745a47c18db297afd57b96e183c0583.png)"] [17592186133485 :question/worked-solution "Every linear transformation in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nFrom the diagram we see that\n\n- the image of $\\mathbf{i}$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and\n- the image of $\\mathbf{j}$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186133484 :question/problem "The linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ has the standard matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhat are the images of the standard basis vectors $\\{\\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j} \\}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$?"] [17592186133484 :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nThe images of the vectors in the standard basis $\\{\\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j} \\}$ are the first and second columns of $T$, respectively.\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(i) &= T\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(j) &= T\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the images of the vectors in the standard basis $\\{\\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j} \\}$ by computing the matrix products $T\\cdot\\mathbf{i}$ and $T\\cdot\\mathbf{j}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(i) &= T \\cdot i = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(j) &= T \\cdot j = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186132579 17592186132580 17592186132581 17592186132582 17592186132583] "immutable_fields" [[17592186132578 :answer-field/correct] [17592186132578 :answer-field/id] [17592186132578 :answer-field/key] [17592186132578 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
